From a05fa983fb4699dc1dc704776e3fed014c215cc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mecattaf <thomas@mecattaf.dev>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 02:07:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] [ROCm] Survive the 7.14 overlapping host-registration
 trap on the PLE path

Measured on this stack, not inherited: ds4's rocm_host_copy_probe was run
inside flashnext:dev on 2026-08-31 and passes ONLY in
--expect-overlap-rejected mode (RUN3-BRIEF 16.1). Reproduced again in
process while writing this commit: registering two page-overlapping spans
of one mapping returns 712 (hipErrorHostMemoryAlreadyRegistered), and a
plain hipMemcpy(H2D) out of the rejected pointer then fails with
"CUDA error: invalid argument" / hipErrorInvalidValue.

Both halves matter and the second is the one that is easy to miss:
detecting the registration error is not sufficient, because the ordinary
copy is poisoned too. The registration is PAGE-ROUNDED -- base down,
length up -- so two logically DISJOINT slices of one mmap collide as soon
as their rounded spans share a page. With a ~51.2 GB engram table sliced
into non-page-aligned spans that is the common case, not an edge case
(15.1). It shows up at model LOAD, as a failure or a silent fallback,
never as slow decode.

Ported by shape (not by copy) from ds4-strix-halo-tp-odinlink commit
8d45d16, rocm/ds4_rocm_runtime.cuh:5513-5600 (cuda_model_range_ptr).

common/ple_mmap.py -- new "ROCm 7.14 overlapping host registration"
section:
  page_rounded_span()  ds4's rounding arithmetic at :5518-5523, kept in
      shape because the rounding is what manufactures the collision.
  mapping_span()       the mitigation 16.1 points at: np.memmap already
      maps from a page-aligned start covering exactly the pages the array
      needs, so registering THAT extent is the same pages with none of the
      rounding, and cannot pull a neighbouring slice's page in. Probe rows
      page-aligned-register and anonymous-mmap-register both pass cleanly;
      the four shard mappings of a synthetic table register with zero
      collisions on the real device.
  MmapPleTable.host_register()  registers the shard mappings and is never
      fatal. A rejected span is the EXPECTED 7.14 outcome, so it latches
      table.overlapping, logs why, and the load continues.
  MmapPleTable.to_device()  the ordinary path is still the plain copy and
      pays nothing. A latched table stages; an unlatched one tries the
      plain copy and latches on the specific failure a rejected
      registration produces -- which does not need THIS table to have
      registered anything, since a page-adjacent registration anywhere in
      the process poisons copies out of these pages. Anything else
      re-raises.
  staged_copy_to_device()  the fallback loop of :5566 onward. Three
      properties of that fix are load-bearing and are pinned by tests: the
      pinned bounce is allocated ONLY on the overlap path, so the ordinary
      path pays nothing; it is capped at 64 MiB however large the copy is,
      so the extra RSS is bounded; and it is freed on success AND on every
      error exit (resize_(0), so the block is gone at that statement
      rather than whenever the caching host allocator gets round to it).
  _latch_mapping_unsupported()  the second, silent failure mode at :5548.
      cudaErrorNotSupported / cudaErrorInvalidValue disables host mapping
      for the WHOLE process and ds4 says nothing about it. Logged at ERROR
      with the word LATCHED, because a warning is invisible in a load's
      log volume and this one is permanent until the seat restarts.

close() unregisters every span it registered, before dropping the memmaps
those addresses live in.

Kill switch: VLLM_PLE_MMAP_REGISTER, default 0. The registration is the
new mechanism and stays opt-in; the staged-copy fallback is always armed,
because the poisoned-copy failure does not require us to have registered
anything.

Adds tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_host_registration.py (30 tests,
GPU-free): the rounding arithmetic and an executable statement of 15.1's
collision claim, the alignment mitigation, rejection-is-survivable,
the process-wide latch and its loud log, the bounce's overlap-only
allocation / 64 MiB cap / free on both exits, the poisoned-copy latch,
the no-swallow path for unrelated errors, and the knob's default.

Verified in flashnext:dev on gfx1151 (HIP 7.14.60850, torch
2.13.0+rocm7.14.0): 30 new tests pass; test_ple_mmap.py (82),
test_ple_mmap_amd.py (15) and test_ple.py (11) still pass; ruff check and
format clean; tools/check_nix_substrate.py passes. A synthetic table on
the real device registers 4 spans with no collision, a deliberate
colliding re-registration returns 712, and the staged copy returns
byte-exact rows.

